hadiths[12]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1690","Ziyad ibn al-Rabi Abu Khidash told us: Wasil, freedman of Abu Uyaynah, told us, from Bashshar ibn Abi Sayf al-Jarmi, from Iyad ibn Ghutayf, he said: We entered upon Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah visiting him for an illness that had afflicted him, while his wife Tuhayfah was sitting at his head. I said: How did Abu Ubaydah spend the night. She said: By Allah, he spent the night with reward. Abu Ubaydah said: I did not spend the night with reward, and he was facing the wall with his face, then he turned to the people with his face and said: Will you not ask me about what I said. They said: We did not admire what you said such that we ask you about it. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Whoever spends a surplus spending in the cause of Allah, it is by seven hundred; and whoever spends upon himself and his family, or visits a sick person, or removes harm, the good deed is by ten like it; and fasting is a shield as long as he does not tear it; and whomever Allah afflicts with an affliction in his body, it is an expiation for him."
"1691","Yahya ibn Said told us: Ibrahim ibn Maymun told us: Sad ibn Samurah ibn Jundub told us, from his father, from Abu Ubaydah, he said: The last words the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spoke: Expel the Jews of the people of Hijaz and the people of Najran from the Arabian Peninsula, and know that the worst of people are those who take the graves of their prophets as mosques."
"1692","Muhammad ibn Jafar told us: Shubah told us, from Khalid, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Abdullah ibn Suraqah, from Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he mentioned the Dajjal and described him with a description I do not remember. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how will our hearts be that day, as today. He said: Or better."
"1693","Affan and Abd al-Samad said: Hammad ibn Salamah told us: Khalid al-Hadhdha informed us, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Abdullah ibn Suraqah, from Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: There was no prophet after Nuh but that he warned his people of the Dajjal, and I warn you of him. He said: So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, described him to us. He said: And perhaps some who saw me or heard my speech will meet him. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how will our hearts be that day, like them today. He said: Or better."
"1694","Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri told us: Ibrahim ibn Maymun told us, from Sad ibn Samurah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, from Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, he said: The last words the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spoke: Expel the Jews of Hijaz from the Arabian Peninsula, and know that the worst of people are those who take graves as mosques."
"1695","Ismail ibn Umar told us: Israil told us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Walid ibn Abi Malik, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, he said: A man of the Muslims granted protection to a man, while over the army was Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. Khalid ibn al-Walid and Amr ibn al-As said: We will not grant him protection. Abu Ubaydah said: We grant him protection; I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Any one of the Muslims grants protection over the Muslims."
"1696","Abu al-Mughirah told us: Safwan ibn Amr told us: Abu Hisbah Muslim ibn Ukays, freedman of Abdullah ibn Amir, told us, from Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, he said: He mentioned one who entered upon him and found him weeping. He said: What makes you weep, O Abu Ubaydah. He said: We weep that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, mentioned one day what Allah will open upon the Muslims and bestow upon them, until he mentioned Syria. He said: If your term is lengthened, O Abu Ubaydah, three servants suffice you: a servant to serve you, a servant to travel with you, and a servant to serve your family and return to them; and three mounts suffice you: a mount for your saddle, a mount for your baggage, and a mount for your boy. Then this is me looking at my house filled with slaves, and looking at my stable filled with mounts and horses. So how shall I meet the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, after this, when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, enjoined upon us: The most beloved of you to me and nearest of you to me is he who meets me upon the like of the state I left him upon."
"1697","Yaqub told us: My father told us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: Aban ibn Salih narrated to me, from Shahr ibn Hawshab al-Ashari, from Rabbihi, a man of his people who had succeeded to his mother after his father, who had witnessed the plague of Amwas, he said: When the pain flared up, Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah stood among the people as a preacher and said: O people, this pain is the mercy of your Lord and the supplication of your Prophet and the death of the righteous before you; and Abu Ubaydah asks Allah to apportion for him his share of it. He said: So he was struck and died, may Allah have mercy on him. And Muadh ibn Jabal was appointed over the people, and he stood as a preacher after him and said: O people, this pain is the mercy of your Lord and the supplication of your Prophet and the death of the righteous before you; and Muadh asks Allah to apportion for the family of Muadh their share of it. He said: So his son Abd al-Rahman ibn Muadh was struck and died. Then he stood and supplicated his Lord for himself, and he was struck in his palm; I saw him looking at it then kissing the back of his hand then saying: I would not love to have for what is in you anything of this world. When he died, Amr ibn al-As was appointed over the people, and he stood among us as a preacher and said: O people, when this pain occurs it only flares like the flaring of fire, so take refuge from it in the mountains. Abu Wathilah al-Hudhali said to him: You have lied; by Allah, I accompanied the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while you are worse than this donkey of mine. He said: By Allah, I will not answer what you say; by Allah, we shall not stay upon it. Then he left and the people left, and they dispersed from it, and Allah repelled it from them. He said: And that reached Umar ibn al-Khattab, of the view of Amr; by Allah, he did not dislike it. Abu Abd al-Rahman Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal said: Aban ibn Salih is the grandfather of Abu Abd al-Rahman Mushkudanah."
"1698","Muhammad ibn Abi Adi told us, from Dawud, from Amir, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent the army of Dhat al-Salasil, and he appointed Abu Ubaydah over the Muhajirun and appointed Amr ibn al-As over the Bedouins, and said to them: Obey one another. He said: And they were commanded to raid Bakr. So Amr set out and raided Qudaah because Bakr were his maternal uncles. Then al-Mughirah ibn Shubah went to Abu Ubaydah and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, appointed you over us, and the son of so-and-so has taken over the affair of the people, and you have no command with him. Abu Ubaydah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded us to obey one another, so I obey the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, even if Amr disobeys him."
"1699","Waki told us: Ibrahim ibn Maymun, freedman of the family of Samurah, narrated to me, from Ishaq ibn Sad ibn Samurah, from his father, from Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, he said: The last words the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spoke: Expel the Jews of the people of Hijaz and the people of Najran from the Arabian Peninsula."
"1700","Yazid ibn Harun told us: Hisham informed us, from Wasil, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Iyad ibn Ghutayf, he said: We entered upon Abu Ubaydah visiting him. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Whoever spends a surplus spending in the cause of Allah, it is by seven hundred; and whoever spends upon himself or upon his family, or visits a sick person, or removes harm from the path, it is a good deed by ten like it; and fasting is a shield as long as he does not tear it; and whomever Allah afflicts with an affliction in his body, it is an expiation for him."
"1701","Yazid narrated to us, Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, Bashshar ibn Abi Sayf narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abdur-Rahman, from Iyad ibn Ghutayf who said: We entered upon Abu Ubaydah and he mentioned the hadith."
